{
  "nbformat": 4,
  "nbformat_minor": 5,
  "metadata": {
    "kernelspec": {
      "name": "maxima",
      "display_name": "Maxima",
      "language": "maxima"
    },
    "aximar": {}
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Robot Arm Kinematics\n\nThis notebook works through the kinematics of a two-link planar robot arm, the geometry behind every pick-and-place robot. We derive the forward kinematics and the Jacobian symbolically, find the configurations where the arm loses a direction of motion, and solve the inverse problem in closed form. The closed-form solution moves the arm along a path.\n\nA numerical Newton solver then reaches the same answers iteratively, as a controller would for an arm too complex for a closed form, and a final section shows why joint speeds blow up as the arm approaches a singular configuration.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Forward Kinematics [S]\n\nEach joint rotates the rest of the arm and each link translates along its own axis. In homogeneous coordinates a rotation by $\\theta$ followed by a translation $l$ along the rotated $x$ axis is the matrix\n\n$$A(\\theta, l) = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos\\theta & -\\sin\\theta & l\\cos\\theta \\\\ \\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta & l\\sin\\theta \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nThe end effector's position is the last column of $A(\\theta_1, l_1)\\,A(\\theta_2, l_2)$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "A(th, l) := matrix([cos(th), -sin(th), l*cos(th)],\n                   [sin(th),  cos(th), l*sin(th)],\n                   [0, 0, 1])$\nT02 : trigreduce(expand(A(theta1, l1) . A(theta2, l2)))$\nfk : [T02[1, 3], T02[2, 3]];",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ l_{2}\\,\\cos \\left(\\theta_{2}+\\theta_{1}\\right)+l_{1}\\,\\cos \\theta_{1} , l_{2}\\,\\sin \\left(\\theta_{2}+\\theta_{1}\\right)+l_{1}\\,\\sin \\theta_{1} \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 2,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The result is the familiar pair $x = l_1\\cos\\theta_1 + l_2\\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2)$, $y = l_1\\sin\\theta_1 + l_2\\sin(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2)$. The elbow joint sits at the end of the first link.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "elbow : [l1*cos(theta1), l1*sin(theta1)]$\nlinks : [l1 = 1.0, l2 = 0.7]$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The Jacobian and Singularities [S]\n\nThe Jacobian $J = \\partial(x, y)/\\partial(\\theta_1, \\theta_2)$ maps joint velocities to end-effector velocity, $\\dot{\\mathbf{x}} = J\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\theta}$. Where its determinant vanishes the arm cannot move in some direction, however fast its joints turn.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "J : jacobian(fk, [theta1, theta2])$\ndetJ : trigsimp(trigexpand(determinant(J)))$\n[J, 'determinant('J) = detJ];",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ \\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined\\pmatrix{\\else\\begin{pmatrix}\\fi -\\left( l_{2}\\,\\sin \\left(\\theta_{2}+\\theta_{1}\\right)\\right)-l_{1}\\,\\sin \\theta_{1}& -\\left(l_{2}\\,\\sin \\left(\\theta_{2}+\\theta_{1}\\right)\\right)\\cr l_{2}\\,\\cos \\left(\\theta_{2}+\\theta_{1}\\right)+l_{1}\\,\\cos \\theta_{1}&l_{2}\\,\\cos \\left( \\theta_{2}+\\theta_{1}\\right)\\cr \\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined}\\else\\end{pmatrix}\\fi  , \\det J=l_{1}\\,l_{2}\\,\\sin \\theta_{2} \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 4,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The determinant $l_1 l_2 \\sin\\theta_2$ vanishes at $\\theta_2 = 0$, where the arm is fully stretched, and at $\\theta_2 = \\pi$, where it is folded back on itself. Neither depends on $\\theta_1$. Both lie on the edge of the workspace, so the boundary of what the arm can reach is exactly where it loses mobility.\n\n## The Workspace [N]\n\nThe tip can reach any point whose distance from the base lies between $|l_1 - l_2| = 0.3$ and $l_1 + l_2 = 1.7$. Sampling joint angles uniformly shows the annulus and how the reachable points crowd towards its edges, a sign of the singularities there.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "fk_num : subst(links, fk)$\nfk_f : buildq([e : fk_num], lambda([t1, t2], float(subst([theta1 = t1, theta2 = t2], e))))$\nsamples : create_list(fk_f(t1, t2),\n  t1, makelist(2*%pi*i/60, i, 0, 59),\n  t2, makelist(2*%pi*j/60, j, 0, 59))$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"steelblue\", marker_size=2, opacity=0.4, name=\"Reachable points\",\n  points(map(first, samples), map(second, samples)),\n  color=\"black\", name=\"r = 0.3\", parametric(0.3*cos(u), 0.3*sin(u), u, 0, 2*%pi),\n  color=\"black\", name=\"r = 1.7\", parametric(1.7*cos(u), 1.7*sin(u), u, 0, 2*%pi),\n  aspect_ratio=true, title=\"Workspace of the Two-Link Arm (l1 = 1.0, l2 = 0.7)\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"y\", showlegend=false\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "application/x-maxima-plotly": [
