    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Inverse Kinematics in Closed Form [S]\n\nGiven a target $(x, y)$, the law of cosines in the triangle formed by the two links and the line from the base to the target gives the elbow angle:\n\n$$\\cos\\theta_2 = \\frac{x^2 + y^2 - l_1^2 - l_2^2}{2 l_1 l_2}$$\n\nBoth signs of $\\theta_2$ work. For a target above the $x$ axis, positive $\\theta_2$ puts the elbow below the line from the base to the target (elbow down) and negative $\\theta_2$ puts it above (elbow up). The shoulder angle is the direction to the target less the angle the forearm adds:\n\n$$\\theta_1 = \\operatorname{atan2}(y, x) - \\operatorname{atan2}(l_2\\sin\\theta_2,\\; l_1 + l_2\\cos\\theta_2)$$",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "ik(x, y, sgn) := block([c2, th2],\n  c2 : (x^2 + y^2 - l1^2 - l2^2)/(2*l1*l2),\n  th2 : sgn*acos(c2),\n  [atan2(y, x) - atan2(l2*sin(th2), l1 + l2*cos(th2)), th2])$\n\n/* Numeric version: bind the link lengths first so the arithmetic stays in floats */\nik_num(x, y, sgn) := block([l1 : 1.0, l2 : 0.7], float(ik(x, y, sgn)))$\n\n/* Check both solutions on a few targets */\ntargets : [[1.2, 0.5], [0.4, 1.1], [-0.9, 0.6], [0.5, -0.8]]$\nfor p in targets do block([q_pos, q_neg, err],\n  q_pos : ik_num(p[1], p[2], 1),\n  q_neg : ik_num(p[1], p[2], -1),\n  err : max(abs(fk_f(q_pos[1], q_pos[2]) - p)[1], abs(fk_f(q_pos[1], q_pos[2]) - p)[2],\n            abs(fk_f(q_neg[1], q_neg[2]) - p)[1], abs(fk_f(q_neg[1], q_neg[2]) - p)[2]),\n  printf(true, \"target (~5,2f, ~5,2f): theta = (~6,3f, ~6,3f) or (~6,3f, ~6,3f), error ~,1e~%\",\n         p[1], p[2], q_pos[1], q_pos[2], q_neg[1], q_neg[2], err))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "target ( 1.20,  0.50): theta = (-0.167,  1.427) or ( 0.957, -1.427), error 2.2e-16\ntarget ( 0.40,  1.10): theta = ( 0.584,  1.657) or ( 1.860, -1.657), error 1.1e-16\ntarget (-0.90,  0.60): theta = ( 1.872,  1.801) or ( 3.235, -1.801), error 2.2e-16\ntarget ( 0.50, -0.80): theta = (-1.747,  2.014) or (-0.277, -2.014), error 2.2e-16"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Tracing a Path [S+N]\n\nThe tip traces a circle of radius 0.35 centred on $(0.9, 0.4)$, which stays inside the workspace, using the elbow-down solution. Because the inverse kinematics has a closed form, the joint angles are explicit functions of time, and so are the positions of the elbow and the tip. The plot substitutes twelve values of $t$ into those expressions and overlays the arm at each, fading from the first pose to the last.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "path : [0.9 + 0.35*cos(t), 0.4 + 0.35*sin(t)]$\nq_t : subst(links, ik(path[1], path[2], 1))$\nelbow_t : subst(links, subst([theta1 = q_t[1]], elbow))$\n\npose(tk) := float(subst(t = tk, [[0, elbow_t[1], path[1]], [0, elbow_t[2], path[2]]]))$\nposes : makelist(pose(2*%pi*k/12), k, 0, 11)$\n\napply(ax_draw2d, append(\n  [color = \"lightgray\", dash = \"dash\", parametric(path[1], path[2], t, 0, 2*%pi),\n   color = \"lightgray\", parametric(1.7*cos(u), 1.7*sin(u), u, 0, 2*%pi),\n   dash = \"solid\"],\n  apply(append, makelist(\n    [color = \"steelblue\", line_width = 3, opacity = 0.2 + 0.8*k/11,\n     lines(poses[k + 1][1], poses[k + 1][2]),\n     color = \"black\", marker_size = 6,\n     points(poses[k + 1][1], poses[k + 1][2])], k, 0, 11)),\n  [aspect_ratio = true, xrange = [-0.5, 1.8], yrange = [-0.6, 1.4],\n   title = \"Two-Link Arm Tracing a Circle\", xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"y\",\n   showlegend = false]))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
