    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Inverse Kinematics by Newton's Method [N]\n\nMost arms have no closed-form inverse, and controllers solve $f(\\boldsymbol\\theta) = \\mathbf{x}_{\\text{target}}$ numerically. Newton's method linearises with the Jacobian at each step:\n\n$$\\boldsymbol\\theta_{k+1} = \\boldsymbol\\theta_k + J(\\boldsymbol\\theta_k)^{-1}\\left(\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{target}} - f(\\boldsymbol\\theta_k)\\right)$$\n\nStarting from a rough guess, the error falls quadratically: each step roughly squares it.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "J_num : subst(links, J)$\nJ_f : buildq([e : J_num], lambda([t1, t2], float(subst([theta1 = t1, theta2 = t2], e))))$\n\nnewton_ik(target, q0, steps) := block([q : q0, err],\n  for k : 1 thru steps do (\n    err : target - fk_f(q[1], q[2]),\n    printf(true, \"step ~d: error ~,3e~%\", k - 1, sqrt(err[1]^2 + err[2]^2)),\n    q : q + np_to_list(np_flatten(np_solve(ndarray(J_f(q[1], q[2])),\n                                           ndarray(transpose(matrix(err))))))),\n  q)$\n\nq_newton : newton_ik([1.2, 0.5], [0.5, 1.0], 6)$\nprint(\"Newton:  \", q_newton)$\nprint(\"Analytic:\", ik_num(1.2, 0.5, 1))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "step 0: error 7.306e-1\nstep 1: error 3.014e-1\nstep 2: error 5.341e-2\nstep 3: error 1.371e-3\nstep 4: error 1.372e-6\nstep 5: error 9.653e-13\nNewton:   [-0.16727868330586573,1.4274487578895314]\nAnalytic: [-0.1672786833058656,1.4274487578895312]"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Newton's method converges to the solution with positive $\\theta_2$ because the starting guess has a positive elbow angle. A guess with a negative elbow angle finds the other solution.\n\n## Approaching a Singularity [S+N]\n\nSuppose the tip moves straight out along the $x$ axis at unit speed towards full reach. The joint velocities needed are $\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\theta} = J^{-1}\\dot{\\mathbf{x}}$, and $J^{-1}$ contains $1/\\det J = 1/(l_1 l_2 \\sin\\theta_2)$. As the arm straightens, $\\sin\\theta_2 \\to 0$ and the joints must spin ever faster.\n\nThe manipulability $w = |\\det J|$ measures how far a configuration is from a singular one.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "r_list : makelist(0.5 + 1.19*i/100, i, 0, 100)$\njoint_speed(r) := block([q, Jq, qd],\n  q : ik_num(r, 0.0, 1),\n  Jq : J_f(q[1], q[2]),\n  qd : np_to_list(np_flatten(np_solve(ndarray(Jq), ndarray(matrix([1.0], [0.0]))))),\n  [sqrt(qd[1]^2 + qd[2]^2), abs(determinant(Jq))])$\nspeeds : map(joint_speed, r_list)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"crimson\", line_width=2, name=\"Joint speed |dθ/dt|\",\n  lines(r_list, map(first, speeds)),\n  color=\"steelblue\", line_width=2, name=\"Manipulability |det J|\",\n  lines(r_list, map(second, speeds)),\n  logy=true, title=\"Joint Speed for Unit Tip Speed Along x\",\n  xlabel=\"Distance from base r\", ylabel=\"value (log scale)\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
