    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "At $r = 1.69$, 1 cm short of full reach, the joints must turn many times faster than in the middle of the workspace to keep the tip moving at the same speed. Robot path planners keep a margin from the workspace boundary for this reason.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Step | Method |\n|------|--------|\n| Forward kinematics | Symbolic: product of homogeneous transforms |\n| Singularities | Symbolic: $\\det J = l_1 l_2 \\sin\\theta_2$ |\n| Workspace | Numeric: sampled joint angles |\n| Inverse kinematics | Symbolic: closed form, two solutions; numeric check of both |\n| Path tracking | Symbolic angles as functions of $t$, evaluated at twelve poses |\n| Newton IK | Numeric: `np_solve` with the Jacobian, quadratic convergence |\n| Near singularity | Numeric: joint speed grows as $1/\\sin\\theta_2$ |",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}